  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A typical summertime pattern prevails Sunday \nthrough the middle of next week, with near average temperatures,\nincreasingly humid conditions, and daily chances of showers and\nthunderstorms.\n\nGOES water vapor channels depict a slow moving upper low over \nthe mid-Mississippi Valley with moist southerly flow ahead of \nthe upper low over the Mid-Atlantic. Surface high pressure is \nnow centered well offshore of the Southeast coast. There is a \nlee-side trough over the Piedmont and low-level SSE flow has \nallowed moisture to pool along that boundary. Given this, some \nisolated showers/tstms have developed in an environment \nfeaturing SBCAPE ~1500 J/kg and low- level lapse rates of \n7.0-7.5 C/km. Mid-level lapse rates are rather poor, so these \nshowers/tstms are not particularly tall. Temperatures are in the\nmid/upper 80s, and slightly more humid than the past few days \nwith dewpoints in the mid 60s to lower 70s. Warm and more humid \ntonight with low temperatures of 70-75F. Mainly dry, with a \nslight chc of showers over the Piedmont and coastal SE VA/NE NC \ntoward morning.\n\nThe pattern becomes more unsettled by Sunday, and this will \ncontinue into early next week as the slow moving upper-level \ntrough approaches from the west. At the same time, a weak cold \nfront approaches from the north, but will remain north of the \nlocal area through Monday (and likely wash out by Tuesday). With\nthe approaching trough and Bermuda high pressure well offshore,\ndeep- layered S to SW flow will prevail. This will allow rich \nGulf moisture to overspread the area on Sunday and remain over \nus early next week. PWs surge to 2.2-2.4\" by later Sunday/Sunday\nnight. Scattered to potentially numerous showers/tstms are \nexpected Sunday. The highest precip chances will initially be \nacross south-central VA during the afternoon (as the deep \nmoisture arrives here first). Showers and tstms should gradually\nspread NE through the evening (though it may very well remain \ndry near the immediate coast and on the Eastern Shore).\n\nShowers/tstms are likely across the entire area on Monday as a \nlead shortwave potentially tracks over the area. Monday appears \nto be the day with the highest convective coverage during the \nperiod. The trough dampens a bit from Tuesday/Wednesday, but \nscattered showers and tstms are still expected during the \naftn/evening on both days as weak SW flow aloft continues and \ndeep moisture remains nearby. Given the environment, and \nrelatively weak unidirectional flow aloft and tall/skinny CAPE, \nthere will be a threat of localized flash flooding each day, but\nthe severe threat should remain relatively low with these \ntstms. Days 2-4 (Sunday through Tuesday) are in a 'Marginal' ERO\nas the overall flash flood risk is more isolated given a lack \nof a larger scale focusing mechanism, as the aforementioned \nfront remains north of the local area. High temperatures dip \ndown slightly below average during the early week period thanks \nto the cloud cover and multiple rounds of showers/tstms. Though \nnot quite as unsettled as the first half of the week, the latter\nhalf of the week will see isolated to scattered afternoon \nshowers and tstms. With the decreased convective coverage \nforecast, temperatures will return to near to slightly above \nnormal with highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s.",
